From a8d9338f4f6b2ecc17cdb82128b89741884c520b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
Date: Fri, 10 Jul 2026 00:41:01 +0000
Subject: usb: xhci: tolerate COMP_CTX_STATE in abort_td's final completion
 check

abort_td() issues a Stop Ring command followed by a Set TR Dequeue
Pointer command to cancel a pending transfer. The completion check
for the Stop Ring command already tolerates COMP_CTX_STATE as an
acceptable outcome alongside COMP_SUCCESS. The completion check for
the following Set TR Dequeue Pointer command did not, and hit its
BUG_ON when the endpoint had transitioned to a state where that
command legitimately returns COMP_CTX_STATE instead.

Observed on hardware: this panicked during normal interrupt endpoint
polling, on the second poll after the first one's timeout triggered
this same abort path successfully.

Fix: apply the same tolerance already used for the earlier check in
this function, and log when it happens (dev_dbg) purely for future
diagnostic visibility - no behavior change.

Verified this is safe, not just tolerated blind: Linux's xhci-hcd
tracks its own software dequeue pointer for a transfer ring
(ep_ring->dequeue) and only updates it after confirming the completed
Set TR Dequeue Pointer command's target matches what it queued -
otherwise it warns and leaves that tracking untouched, but still
proceeds with TD cleanup and restarts the ring regardless (Linux does
not halt or error out to the caller over this completion code
either). That extra care exists specifically to protect Linux's own
software dequeue tracking from going stale.

This driver has no equivalent tracking to protect: grepping every
read/write of a transfer ring's ->dequeue/->deq_seg fields in this
driver shows they're written once at ring allocation and never read
again - inc_deq(), the only function that advances a ring's dequeue
pointer, is called exactly once in the whole driver, only for
ctrl->event_ring (a different ring entirely), never for a per-endpoint
transfer ring. The fields that are actually live for a transfer ring
(->enqueue, ->cycle_state) are maintained entirely by this driver's
own enqueue-side bookkeeping (inc_enq()) and are completely
unaffected by what this completion code says either way. So the
specific risk this tolerance was flagged for - the driver silently
assuming the dequeue pointer moved somewhere it didn't - cannot
happen here: nothing in this driver reads that field for a transfer
ring to have a wrong assumption about in the first place. Linux's
more careful handling solves a problem that doesn't exist in this
codebase's design, rather than being a protection this driver is
missing.

Signed-off-by: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
---
 drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
index 00344e8074..9707988c3b 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-ring.c
@@ -594,9 +594,12 @@ static void abort_td(struct usb_device *udev, int ep_index)
 	if (!event)
 		return;
 
+	comp = GET_COMP_CODE(le32_to_cpu(event->event_cmd.status));
+	if (comp == COMP_CTX_STATE)
+		dev_dbg(ctrl->dev, "abort_td: Set TR Dequeue Pointer got CTX_STATE, endpoint was already in the target state\n");
 	BUG_ON(TRB_TO_SLOT_ID(le32_to_cpu(event->event_cmd.flags))
-		!= udev->slot_id || GET_COMP_CODE(le32_to_cpu(
-		event->event_cmd.status)) != COMP_SUCCESS);
+		!= udev->slot_id || (comp != COMP_SUCCESS && comp
+		!= COMP_CTX_STATE));
 	xhci_acknowledge_event(ctrl);
 }
 
